moudgill@banjo.cs.cornell.edu (Mayan Moudgill) writes:

>How can single combat be simulated? ...

>I've tried to come up with some kind of a system. The basic 
>parameters are
>1] weapon position (high-left, center)
>2] balance (forward, centered)
>3] body position (jumping, kneel, stand, sprawl)
>4] violence of move.(thrust vs. lunge)
>5] point attack vs. edge attack.     >mayan >moudgill@cs.cornell.edu

To model single combat, you'd have to capture three different
kinds of characteristics of individuals:

(1) What is physically possible - a result of physical limitations,
    laws of dynamics, gravity/balance, speed and impulse. Responses
    as forces movement and getting "hit" are also relevant. Tiredness...

(2) What is behaviourally possible - ie, what the individual has been
    trained to accomplish (including timing details and psychological
    factors. If the situation means X, what does the trained individual
    do about it... (see 3 below).

Both of these are constraints on the individuals. Not deterministic, as
many possible (moves, signs, ...) are possible - only one is "chosen".

(3) Perception - what the individual is capable of detecting, predicting
    ahead, getting surprised about, being tricked by, ...

Machine simulation of these models would be a fairly intricate task.
Probably a frames approach based on kata and drill, AND need to be
somewhat unpredictable. Look ahead games trees would sit on this
too, I s'pose...

Realistic physical models can be done with a pin-jointed frame model 
with centre of masses and moments of inertia plus models of muscle 
co-ordination (but this would be difficult) - an easier approach 
would be a look-up table with possible/timedelay/impulse/accuracy and 
some model for likely damage effects.

Perception models would be really difficult - it's not just what CAN
be seen, but what is ACTUALLY seen, and how it's interpretted.

I would also include some machine learning (at least parameter
adjustment for credit assignment)...

BUT, anything less than this would be wimpy in my opinion.

